User: Can you explain how a for loop works in C++?

TA: A `for` loop in C++ has three parts in its header — an initializer, a condition, and an increment — and executes its body repeatedly until the condition is false [1](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-s096-introduction-to-c-and-c-january-iap-2013/pages/lectures-and-assignments/):

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    // body executes while i < 10
}
```

If you want to explore the theory behind control flow more deeply, toggle "Study Assist" mode — but what specific part of your current assignment are you trying to use a loop for?
